# Model Selection for Sub-Agents

## Purpose

Match the model to the work. A reviewer scanning a twelve-line diff and a
regression reviewer reasoning across a forty-file blast radius should not run on
the same model: the cheap work pays flagship prices, and the hard work gets no
more capability than the trivial.

## Tiers, not model names

A skill declares a **tier**. It never declares a model.

A model name in a skill is wrong the day the provider changes and unusable for
anyone on a different provider. There are three tiers:

| Tier | For |
|---|---|
| `light` | mechanical, verifiable work — pre-filter, `class_scope` existence checks, comment collection, formatting a reply |
| `standard` | ordinary reviewing and implementation — the default |
| `deep` | genuinely hard reasoning — regression review across the blast radius, a strategy change after a failed loop, synthesis across many findings |

Declared in SKILL.md frontmatter:

```yaml
---
name: review-architecture
model_tier: deep
---
```

An undeclared skill runs at `standard`. A skill that writes a model name into
`model_tier` fails the guard in `src/gdskills/model-tier.test.ts`.

## Resolving a tier: discover, rank, fall back

There is **no table of models** anywhere in the resolution, and there must not
be. A fixed list of model ids is stale the day a provider ships anything, and it
is wrong for every operator whose environment differs from the one it was written
in. The candidate set is discovered at runtime.

`src/commands/select.ts` already detects providers and every `DetectedProvider`
carries `models: string[]`. That list — for the **session's own provider**, so a
child never resolves onto a provider the parent holds no grant for — is the whole
candidate set. `src/gdskills/model-tier.ts` then:

1. **discovers** the candidates (passed in, never looked up: the module reads no
   network, no filesystem, no environment);
2. **ranks** them by size markers in their names — `MODEL_RANK_HINTS`;
3. **places the tiers relative to the session's own model**:

| Tier | Resolves to |
|---|---|
| `standard` | the session's model, always |
| `deep` | the best discovered model **above** the session's — a larger size class, or a NEWER generation of the session model's own family — else the session's |
| `light` | the **next size step below** the session's (an Opus-class session takes the Sonnet class, not the Haiku class), else the session's; the smallest class only when nothing sits between |

Anchoring on the session model is what makes "never a downgrade" checkable
rather than hoped for: a tier can only move away from the session model in the
direction its own name points, and a candidate at the session's own rank is a
lateral move and never taken. An OLDER sibling of the session's own family is
never `light` or `deep` either.

## What the hints are, and what they are not

Capability cannot be derived from a bare string — `haiku` is smaller than `opus`
and nothing about the two strings says so. `MODEL_RANK_HINTS` is that irreducible
residue, and it is kept in the smallest shape that works:

- it names **size words** (`mini`, `lite`, `flash`, `haiku`, `pro`, `opus`,
  `max`, `ultra`, …), never models, so it claims nothing about what exists and
  cannot go stale;
- it is applied to whatever detection reported, so an unfamiliar vendor is still
  ranked if its names use those words;
- a model matching **no** hint is *unranked*, which is not the same as ranked
  zero — it is simply not placed;
- it is one array, overridable per call.

A model whose name is a codename carrying no size is unrankable by design. That
is the honest outcome, not a gap to be patched with folklore.

**Version is a second axis, not a size hint (flow 358).** `rankModelId` still
never parses a version number out of an id: the size words order size CLASSES.
Generation is ordered separately, and only within **the same family and
vendor** — the id with its version tokens removed (`familyKey`) must match. A
newer release outranks an older one of its own family, so a session on the
older one resolves `deep` to the newer sibling, and a session on the newer one
never resolves `light` or `deep` to the older. A version number is **never**
compared across families: a higher number on one line says nothing about a
different line.

**A larger class of an older generation is ambiguous, not "above".** When the
only discovered models above the session are a larger size class whose version is
older than the session's, size words alone cannot call them stronger, and
`deep` must not move onto a model that is older-generation and — by the
profile price — the more expensive one. Without an agent the tier keeps the
session's model as `session-fallback`, with the reason recorded (reason id
`kept-older-generation`, or `kept-older-generation-pricier` when the profile
price shows it costs more). The agent fallback below is the only other way out.

The routing "derived default table" (`deriveDefaultTable`,
`src/harness/routing/derive-default-table.ts`, flow 327/PRD §6.3) is that
different consumer: it picks one model per routing category (`review`,
`subagents`, `quick`, …) from a whole connected catalogue, not a tier
relative to the session. The operator decided, 2026-09-25 (review of PR #718:
a session's newer release lost a tie to an older release of the SAME family
it should have beaten), that WITHIN the same family and vendor a newer
version ranks higher there — the family word (a size marker from this same
`MODEL_RANK_HINTS` list) already carries the size class, so version is free
to order generations within it. That table reuses
`rankModelId`/`MODEL_RANK_HINTS` UNMODIFIED for the size axis and adds its
own separate, conservative version parse on top of it. Since flow 358 the tier
resolution reads the same version parse (`src/gdskills/model-version.ts`, shared
so neither module imports the other).

That version parse (`parseModelVersion`/`familyKey`, round 2) reads BOTH a
dotted id (`vendor-opus-5.5`, `gemini-3.8-flash`) and a real
Anthropic-style HYPHENATED one, where a run of 2-3 adjacent short (1-2
digit) numeric tokens reads as a dotted version (`vendor-opus-4-8` → `4.8`).
It still refuses a date/snapshot-shaped token (6-8 bare digits) and more
than one numeric group in the same id — no version rather than a guess. A
`gpt-4o`-style id (a short number plus one trailing letter) parses the
number alone as the version, the letter kept only as an ignored variant
tag, so `gpt-4o` and `gpt-4.1` compare within the same family instead of
never comparing at all. Two versions of the same family are never decided
by alphabetical order — the id-string fallback only applies when neither
side has a parseable version.

**A parameter-size token is never a version (round 3, review of PR #718).**
`7b`, `32b`, `70b`, `1.5b`, `8x7b` — a total or MoE "N experts x M billion"
param count — always stay in `familyKey` and are never fed to
`parseModelVersion`. Before this fix, the letter-variant shape meant for
`gpt-4o` (`/^(\d{1,2})([a-z])$/`, any trailing lowercase letter) also
matched a size token, so `qwen2.5-coder-7b` and `qwen2.5-coder-32b` were
misread as the SAME family at "version" 7 vs. 32 — merging two genuinely
different-sized models and ranking the smaller one "newer". The letter
variant is now restricted to the one real vendor shape that needs it
(`4o`'s trailing `o`); every other trailing letter after a short digit run
is a size suffix, so `qwen2.5-coder-7b`/`qwen2.5-coder-32b` and
`llama-3.3-70b`/`llama-3.3-8b` key to different families and are never
version-compared against each other. A trailing `-latest`/`-preview` alias
word is also stripped from `familyKey` only (never from the id itself), so
`vendor-3-7-sonnet-latest` joins the same family as `vendor-sonnet-5`.

## Falling back

Ranking is **refused**, and every tier keeps the session's provider and model,
when:

- nothing was discovered, or the session's provider is absent from the catalogue
  (an external CLI runtime, or detection that has not run);
- the provider reported no models;
- the hints cannot place the **session's own** model — with no anchor, no
  candidate can be called larger or smaller.

A refusal **never** fails a dispatch and **never** causes a silent downgrade.
Degrading capability because discovery failed is the worst of the three outcomes.

Do not "fix" a fallback by adding model ids to the code. Add a size word to the
hints if one genuinely applies, or leave it: running on the session's model is a
correct answer.

## The agent fallback: comparing candidate models, nothing else

When the ranking is **refused** but the provider did report other models, or a
tier is **ambiguous** (an older-generation larger class, above), an injected
agent may be asked to put the candidate models in order. It is a port
(`TierRankAgent`): `src/gdskills/model-tier.ts` holds no network and no
filesystem, the caller supplies the real one, and a test supplies a fake.

Its limits are the point:

- **It compares candidate models and never rates a task's own difficulty.** It is
  shown no task, no diff and no user text — only the discovered ids and the
  profile prices — and it is asked one question: which of these models is more
  capable than which. The tier is still computed by `assignTier` from signals
  the orchestrator holds.
- It must answer JSON, `{"order": [...]}`, best first, and must place the session's
  own model. An id that was not discovered is dropped. An agent error, a
  timeout, a malformed answer, or one that leaves out the session's model
  resolves to the session's model, exactly as without an agent.
- It never places `standard` or `deep` below the session: `deep` is only ever taken
  from above the session in its order, `standard` is never asked. A larger
  class of an older generation that costs more than the session's model is
  refused whatever it says.
- The result is cached by a hash of the candidate catalogue with prices, so an
  identical catalogue asks once.
- It runs on the **light** tier of the session's own provider — the cheap step
  below the session, or the session model itself when nothing sits below.
- A resolution it produced is recorded `tier_resolution: agent-ranked`.

## Where this actually runs

Two places, and they are not the same shape:

- **`spawn_subagent`** (`src/harness/tool/builtin/spawn-subagent-tool.ts`) takes an
  optional `model_tier` input and does the rest in code: it builds the tier map
  with `buildTierMap` from its host's detection result, hands it to
  `resolveChildModel`, and records the outcome on the dispatch's run trace.
  Omitting `model_tier` inherits the parent's model, exactly as before the field
  existed.
- **An orchestrator authoring a dispatch document** *runs a command*:
  `keryx review tier`. It takes the signals below as flags and prints the `model`
  block ready to paste — the tier, the ordered rule ids that produced it, the
  resolved provider/model, `tier_resolution` and `model_discovery`. `--json`
  prints the block alone.

An orchestrator is an agent following prose and **cannot call a TypeScript
function**: asked for a tier by hand it reads a table of signals and does the
arithmetic in its head — the exact mechanical work this programme moves out of
skills and into the code that consumes them. `keryx review tier` is that code's
entry point; `decideDispatchModel` is what it calls.

Nothing reads a recorded resolution back and checks it. These fields explain a
finished run; they do not gate one.

## A third place: compiling a Claude Code subagent file

`keryx agents export --runtime claude` (`src/agents/compile.ts`'s
`renderClaudeExport`) is neither of the two shapes above — it never asks
`spawn_subagent` or `keryx review tier` anything, and it runs at EXPORT time,
once, against a file written to disk, not at dispatch time. Claude Code's own
subagent frontmatter has no tier concept and no candidate discovery at all; it
accepts exactly four fixed, version-free words for `model` — `opus`, `sonnet`,
`haiku`, `inherit` — and nothing else. The compiler maps a definition's
declared `model_tier` onto that fixed vocabulary directly: `deep` to `opus`,
`standard` to `sonnet`, `light` to `haiku` (an undeclared tier defaults to
`standard`, so it lands on `sonnet`). This is not the discover/rank/fall-back
resolution described above, and it is not a model id — it is a static lookup
into the one enum a host's own docs define, so it can never go stale the way a
literal id would. A project that wants the pre-existing behavior sets
`.metaproject/tasks.config.json`'s `modelGuidance.claudeSubagentAliases:
false`, which restores `model: inherit` for every tier.

## Choosing the tier

The tier is **computed, not judged**. Run:

```bash
keryx review tier --scope blast-radius --findings 12 --diff-lines 340 \
                  --verifier execution --fix-attempt 2 --security \
                  --forced-strategy-change --json
```

Every flag is a signal the orchestrator already holds before it dispatches: the
round's scope, its own attempt counter, the diff it computed, the findings it is
holding, the verification method it is about to ask for. None of them is a
model's self-report — no model is ever asked to rate its own difficulty.

The rules `assignTier` applies, in the order it applies them:

| Signal | Flag | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| scope is `blast-radius` | `--scope blast-radius` | at least `deep` |
| fix attempt >= 2 on the same finding | `--fix-attempt <n>` | raise one tier |
| forced strategy change after the loop cap | `--forced-strategy-change` | `deep` |
| finding count <= 3 **and** diff <= 50 lines | `--findings <n> --diff-lines <n>` | allow `light` |
| verification method is `execution` or `site-check` | `--verifier <method>` | `light` — the evidence comes from running something, not from reasoning |
| any security finding in scope | `--security` | never below `standard` |

Floors are applied after downgrades, so "at least `deep`" means at least: a
blast-radius round over a twelve-line diff is still a blast-radius round.

The session's provider/model come from the **caller**: `--session-provider`/
`--session-model`, else `KERYX_SESSION_PROVIDER`/`KERYX_SESSION_MODEL` exported by
the host (`keryx shell` exports its live session to every `shell_exec` command). The selection `keryx shell` persisted is read only with
`--from-shell-config`: it is whatever `keryx shell` was last pointed at, not the
session running the orchestrator, and as a silent default it pinned a `keryx
shell` model into dispatches authored from Claude Code. The candidate set comes
from live provider detection (`--catalog` to supply it). **There is no `--tier`
flag, and there will not be**: accepting a hand-written tier would put the
arithmetic straight back in the caller's head.

## Adaptive: `inherit: true`

A model is named only when discovery — or, failing that, the agent fallback —
assigned one **other than** the session's (`tier_resolution: discovered` or
`agent-ranked`). In every other case — no session named, ranking
refused, or the tier resolving to the session's own model — the block carries the
tier and `inherit: true`, and the **host** resolves it against the models its own
dispatch tool offers:

| Tier | Host dispatches on |
|---|---|
| `standard` | its session model |
| `deep` | its most capable model class, else the session model |
| `light` | its lighter/faster model class, else the session model |

This is how the tier reaches runtimes keryx cannot enumerate (a Claude Code
`Agent`, a Codex subagent). `light` there is the tier the signals assigned, not a
fallback. Exit status is always 0.

## Recording the decision

The dispatch carries the tier, the ordered rule ids that produced it, which of
the outcomes occurred, and what was on the table when it did —
`model.tier`, `model.tier_reasons` (the assignment rule ids, then `resolve:<reason id>` for the resolution), `model.tier_resolution` and
`model.model_discovery` in
`.metaproject/core/gdskills/contracts/subagent-dispatch.schema.json`.

`tier_resolution` distinguishes four facts that must not be flattened:

| Value | Means |
|---|---|
| `discovered` | a discovered model was assigned to this tier |
| `session-ranked` | ranking worked and placed the tier at the session's model |
| `session-fallback` | ranking was refused or was ambiguous; the session's model is kept |
| `agent-ranked` | the agent fallback put the candidates in order and its answer was used |

"Fell back to the session model" and "assigned the session model because it
ranked there" are different facts. A run that cannot be explained afterwards is
what these fields exist to prevent. Record them on every dispatch that resolves
a tier — `keryx review tier` prints all four together as one pasteable block, so
recording them is one copy rather than four decisions.

## Mandatory behavior

- Declare a tier in the skill; resolve the model at dispatch time.
- **Run `keryx review tier` — do not work the tier out by hand.** Assigning it
  from the table above by reading is the mechanical step this rule exists to
  remove.
- **Do not ask the operator which model to use per dispatch.** Asking every time
  is what made adaptive selection impossible.
- **An interactive review round shows the computed plan once.** It is the third
  start question of `review-orchestrator` (`model_strategy: ask`, the
  interactive default): the complexity the round's signals give, each role's tier
  and the model it resolves to here. The operator accepts it, takes every role to
  `deep`, or sets it — for that round. An unattended round dispatches the
  computed plan without asking (`adaptive`).
- Never write a concrete model name into a skill, a rule, a dispatch template, or
  the resolution code.
- Take the candidate set from runtime detection. Never from a literal list of
  what exists.
- The agent fallback compares candidate models. Never ask any model to rate a
  task's own difficulty, and never let the fallback place `standard` or `deep`
  below the session's model.
- When the candidates cannot be ranked, the block is adaptive (`inherit: true`).
  The host never goes below its session model for `standard` or `deep`, and takes
  a lighter class only for a `light` tier.
